Z||000003||StuGood||12-08-2000||12:01 AM||||Start with the basics.  Check your thermostat (under the cap, in the water outlet housing on the engine).  Is the thermostat in there?  If not, get one and put it in.<P>Next, make sure you have all of the air "burped" out of the cooling system.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||RNGCRSH||12-08-2000||12:13 AM||||Have the radiator checked. Over time they can corrode reducing their ability to cool under load. The engine will idle fine and the temp will look normal. If the stat is stuck shut then it will overheat while at idle. also make sure the fan is comming on when it should.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||12-08-2000||01:11 AM||||I would say your radiator is plugged up.  Let the car run for a bit, then use your hand to check the radiator for cool spots at the front of the radiatior (not the back where the fan is!).  Looking from the front there should be a slight gradual drop from left to right(or right to left.. can't remember).. any drastic changes in temp across the radiatior surface mean that it's pretty corroded on the inside.  <P>Even if you can't feel any big difference, I would take it to a radiator shop, if you can, and have the radiator rodded out.  Shouldn't cost more than 50 bucks and it'll work like new.<P>||||reg||1
